  <article class="article">
    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rental Cost</span>
    <h1>Yamaha Sirius Monthly Rental Price in Hanoi</h1>
    
    <p>A month changes what a rental is. The machine stops being a holiday convenience and becomes daily transport, and the monthly rate is the row of the price list built for that change. At this operator the Yamaha Sirius rents by the month at a verified 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND, a band that costs less than two weeks paid at the weekly rate and roughly a quarter of a month paid daily. For the renter staying long enough to need a routine, the Sirius at the monthly band is the cheapest motorised independence Hanoi offers.</p>
    <p>This guide covers the verified monthly price, the arithmetic that makes a month unbeatable past the first week or so of riding, what the rate includes and what it leaves out, and how to plan the running costs around it. Every figure matches the published <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, the wider machine choices sit in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster, and deposit and paperwork questions are answered in the site <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.</p>

    <nav class="toc"><ol><li><a href="#sec-1">What the monthly rate covers</a></li><li><a href="#sec-2">The verified Yamaha Sirius monthly price</a></li><li><a href="#sec-3">The arithmetic: month against week and day</a></li><li><a href="#sec-4">Why the price is published as a range</a></li><li><a href="#sec-5">Planning a month of running costs</a></li><li><a href="#sec-6">When a month is the right period</a></li><li><a href="#sec-7">Extending or ending a monthly rental</a></li><li><a href="#sec-8">A counter checklist for monthly renters</a></li></ol></nav>

    <section id="sec-1">
      <h2 id="sec-1">What the monthly rate covers</h2>
      <p>The monthly rate covers the Sirius itself for a full rental month, together with what every rate at this operator includes: the machine, a helmet, and the support of the yard for the duration. It is the same semi-automatic underbone the daily and weekly renters ride, priced for the renter who keeps it longer and rides it further. The rate is not a discounted day rate; it is a separate row of the price list, built for the month-long stay.</p>
      <p>What the rate does not cover is everything the city adds. Petrol goes in the tank at your own cost, parking in the busier districts is charged by the session or the day, and any traffic fine is the rider's own affair. A month of riding multiplies these running costs, which is why they get their own section below. None of these extras are hidden; the price list states what the rental includes, and the counter repeats it at handover.</p>
      <p>The monthly renter also gets the one thing short-term renters rarely think about: time. A month is long enough to learn the Sirius's habits, to settle the mirrors and the tyre pressures once and for all, and to treat the machine as transport rather than a novelty. It is also long enough for the rental to settle into a routine: the same machine, the same key, the same spot outside the door, day after day.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-2">
      <h2 id="sec-2">The verified Yamaha Sirius monthly price</h2>
      <p>The published price for a monthly Yamaha Sirius rental at this operator is 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND. The band is the verified figure, and the exact point within it depends on the machine and the terms agreed at the counter, so the right habit is to confirm the precise rate when you sign rather than to assume either end. Treating the band as a floor and a ceiling is more useful than treating it as one number: the floor is what the lucky and the prepared pay, the ceiling is what the cautious should plan around.</p>
      <p>Put beside the other periods, the band tells a clear story. The same machine rents at 150,000 VND per day and 700,000 VND per week. A month at 900,000 VND is less than two weeks at the weekly rate, and at 1,200,000 VND it is still less than two weekly rentals plus a single day. That is the shape of the whole price list: the longer the period, the further the rate falls away from a simple multiple of the daily price.</p>
      <p>The Sirius shares its monthly band with the Honda Wave, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click, so the four cheapest machines on the list are interchangeable at this price. The monthly figure is not a special or a promotion; it is the standing price list, the same list this guide quotes, available to anyone who asks for the month. Renters who want the underbone at its cheapest period start here.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-3">
      <h2 id="sec-3">The arithmetic: month against week and day</h2>
      <p>Take the daily rate first. Thirty days of riding paid at 150,000 VND each would cost 4,500,000 VND, nearly four times the top of the monthly band. Even the disciplined rider who only uses the machine half those days would still spend 2,250,000 VND, close to double the monthly maximum. That is the arithmetic of paying for the wrong period, and it needs no luck or negotiation to avoid, only the right row of the price list.</p>
      <p>The weekly rate narrows the gap but cannot close it. A month is roughly four and a third weeks, so paying by the week costs about 3,000,000 VND before the month is out. Against the 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND band, the weekly payer hands over roughly two to three times what the monthly payer does for the same machine and the same days. The difference is not a rounding error; it is the price of another rented machine, or a month of fuel and parking, spent for nothing. The weekly figures are examined in their own <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/yamaha-sirius-weekly-price/">Yamaha Sirius weekly price</a> guide.</p>
      <p>The break-even sits far earlier than most renters expect. The top of the monthly band, 1,200,000 VND, equals eight daily rentals; the bottom, 900,000 VND, equals six. Any month that includes more than about a week of actual riding is cheaper on the monthly rate, and most months include far more than that. The monthly rate is not for the rider who uses the machine once a week; it is for everyone else, and the savings compound with every trip.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-4">
      <h2 id="sec-4">Why the price is published as a range</h2>
      <p>The site publishes a band for the Sirius rather than a single figure because the monthly rate is the one period where machine condition and rental terms genuinely move the number. A longer rental commits the yard to one machine for weeks, and the agreed terms reflect that commitment. The band is the honest way to show it.</p>
      <p>What the band does not do is invite guesswork. No guide, including this one, can tell you in advance whether your rental will sit at 900,000 VND, at 1,200,000 VND, or between the two. The counter confirms the exact figure when the machine and the dates are fixed, and the rental agreement is where that figure becomes binding.</p>
      <p>The practical rule is therefore simple: budget against the top of the band, hope for the bottom, and treat anything in between as a fair price for a month of honest transport. Renters who plan around the maximum are never surprised at the counter, and surprised renters are the ones who did not. Renters who ask the counter to confirm the figure in writing have done the one thing that makes the band work for them: they have replaced a range with a number.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-5">
      <h2 id="sec-5">Planning a month of running costs</h2>
      <p>Fuel is the first running cost and the Sirius's friendliest one. The machine is built for economy rather than speed, so a month of commuting and errands costs a fraction of what a larger bike would drink. Set a weekly fuel allowance and track it for the first week to calibrate; none of it is charged by the yard, and the fuel pump takes its share directly.</p>
      <p>Parking is the second cost and the one that adds up over thirty days. Anyone keeping the machine a month is usually living somewhere with workable parking, but the city centre is paid territory by the session. Ask at the counter which districts charge and which street parking is free; the answer is local knowledge and it saves real money over a month. Monthly renters also have time to learn which streets are free in the evening and which lots fill by mid-morning, knowledge that daily renters never collect.</p>
      <p>The third cost is maintenance of habit rather than of metal: keeping the chain clean, the tyres hard, and the lights working. A month of Hanoi dust and the occasional downpour is harder on a machine than a weekend of it. The yard supports its rentals, and small issues raised early are small issues solved cheaply. A machine that is checked weekly rides differently from one that is checked never, and the difference shows up in the third week, not the first.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-6">
      <h2 id="sec-6">When a month is the right period</h2>
      <p>The monthly rate fits three kinds of renter. The first is the newcomer settling in: the expat waiting on a shipment, the teacher starting a contract, the student beginning a term. All of them need transport before they need permanence, and all of them ride enough to make the daily rate absurd. For them the monthly band is not a luxury; it is the difference between transport that costs less than a coffee a day and transport that costs more than the trip it enables.</p>
      <p>The second is the traveller who chooses to stay. Hanoi rewards the unhurried, and a month in the city with a Sirius underfoot turns day trips into routine and neighbourhoods into home turf. When the plan is weeks rather than days, the monthly band pays for itself within the first week and a half of riding. The wider long-term options are gathered in the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental Hanoi</a> hub.</p>
      <p>The third is the practical local: the commuter whose own machine is in a shop, the worker on a temporary assignment, the resident between purchases. For all three, the arithmetic in the third section above decides it the same way. More than about a week of riding in a month means the monthly rate wins, and it usually wins by a wide margin. That is why the monthly rate is the quiet recommendation of anyone who has stayed: not because it is cheaper per day, which it is, but because it changes how the city is used.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-7">
      <h2 id="sec-7">Extending or ending a monthly rental</h2>
      <p>A month is a defined period, and the calendar decides when it ends. The date is on the rental agreement, and the date matters: returning late without an agreed extension can turn a cheap month into an expensive lesson, so mark it the day you sign. The calendar also decides when to think about an extension, which is a week before the end, not the day after.</p>
      <p>Extending is a conversation, not an assumption. If the stay grows, ask the yard before the month runs out. The rate for a further month, or for a part-month on top of the first, is set at the counter from the same published figures, and agreeing it early beats negotiating it late. Because the Sirius shares its prices with the Wave at every period, an extension never forces a machine change either.</p>
      <p>Ending is its own small ceremony. Bring the machine back on the agreed date, settle the fuel arrangement made at handover, and let the yard check the machine against the photographs you took on day one. Monthly renters who follow that routine leave with their deposit and their reputation intact, and are remembered kindly when they come back. The habit worth importing from short rentals is the same: photograph everything, agree everything, and leave the rest to the road.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-8">
      <h2 id="sec-8">A counter checklist for monthly renters</h2>
      <p>Six questions at the counter make a month safe. The exact rate within the 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND band, in writing on the agreement. The dates, start and end, plain and fixed. The deposit terms, amount and conditions for its return, in words you can repeat. The fuel arrangement, level in and level out.</p>
      <p>The fifth question is the support channel: who to call if the machine needs attention in week three. The sixth is the extension question: what a longer stay costs and how to arrange it before the month closes. Six answers, five minutes, and a month that behaves. The counter answers all six in the same conversation, and the answers take less time than the ride home.</p>
      <p>Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, rents the Yamaha Sirius by the month at the verified band of 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND. Bring the calendar, ask the six questions, and a month of riding costs less than a fortnight of weekly rental.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>How much does it cost to rent a Yamaha Sirius for a month in Hanoi?</h2><p>At this operator the verified monthly price is a band of 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND. The exact figure within the band is confirmed at the counter when the machine and dates are fixed.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>How much cheaper is a monthly Sirius rental than paying weekly?</h2><p>About four weeks at the 700,000 VND weekly rate comes to roughly 3,000,000 VND. The monthly band of 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND saves between about 1,800,000 and 2,100,000 VND over the same days.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>How many riding days before a monthly rental is cheaper?</h2><p>The top of the monthly band equals eight daily rentals and the bottom equals six. Any month with more than about a week of actual riding is cheaper on the monthly rate.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Does the monthly price include fuel and parking?</h2><p>No. The rate covers the machine, a helmet and the support of the yard. Fuel and parking are running costs on top, and the yard can advise which districts charge for street parking.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>How does the Sirius monthly price compare with other models?</h2><p>The Sirius shares its 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND monthly band with the Honda Wave, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click. The Honda Vision rents monthly at 1,800,000 to 2,000,000 VND and the Honda Air Blade at 1,500,000 VND.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Where can I rent a Yamaha Sirius by the month in Hanoi?</h2><p>Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, rents the Yamaha Sirius by the month and confirms the exact rate within the published band at the counter.</p></article>
    </section>

